  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   Central Idea: Solve the minimum overlap problem using a combination of spectral optimization, Bayesian optimization, and progressive resolution refinement. Unlike previous approaches that relied solely on gradient-based optimization, this method leverages frequency-domain analysis to escape local minima while using Bayesian optimization to aggressively explore promising regions.\n\n   Key Components:\n   - Spectral Representation: Convert candidate solutions to frequency domain to capture periodic patterns that minimize overlap at multiple lags.\n   - Bayesian Optimization: Efficiently explore the solution space using Gaussian Process regression to model the objective function.\n   - Progressive Refinement: Start with coarse representations and incrementally increase resolution to capture finer details that reduce maximum overlap.\n   - Diversity Maintenance: Enforce population diversity through spectral mixing and initialization to avoid premature convergence.\n\n   Why This Approach:\n   - Previous methods got stuck in local optima due to incomplete exploration. The Bayesian optimization framework ensures global exploration.\n   - Frequency-domain analysis identifies patterns that naturally minimize peak correlations.\n   - The adaptive resolution strategy balances exploration cost with solution fidelity.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Representation:\n   - h(x) evaluated at n grid points, x_i = i/n * 2 for i=0,...,n-1.\n   - dx = 2/n\n   - Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n with 0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h_i = n/2.\n\n   Objective:\n   - C_5 = dx * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Discrete approximation: dx * max\u258cnp.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))\n\n   Constraints:\n   - Integral constraint: \u2211h_i = n/2\n   - Box constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n\n   Formulation Innovation:\n   - Augmented Lagrangian in frequency domain: Combine frequency components to enforce integral constraint while optimizing.\n   - Smoothing in frequency domain: Low-pass filter to retain periodic patterns that minimize overlap.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Phase 1: Initialization (Time: <10%)\n   - Create initial population with 5 patterns:\n        * Random projection\n        * Sinusoidal waves\n        * Alternating block patterns\n        * Wavelet-like modulation\n        * Adaptive forcing patterns (period doubling)\n   - Input: n (current resolution) | Output: list of 10 projected candidates\n   \n   Phase 2: Bayesian Optimization (Time: <60%)\n   - Gaussian Process regression with Matern kernel\n   - Acquisition function: Expected Improvement with spectral sensitivity\n   - Each iteration:\n        * Sample new candidate from acquisition function\n        * Apply frequency-domain smoothing (FFT-based)\n        * Project to feasible set\n        * Update GP model with new evaluation\n   - Termination: max(eval_count, time_budget/remaining)\n\n   Phase 3: Local Refinement (Time: 10-20%)\n   - Convert best candidate to constrained optimization problem:\n        min C5(h) s.t. \u2211h_i = n/2\n        0\u2264h_i\u22641\n   - Use SLSQP with 300 iterations\n   - Early termination if no improvement detected\n\n   Phase 4: Resolution Refinement (Time: 10-30%)\n   - Upsample best candidate by factor 2\n   - Re-run Bayesian optimization at new resolution\n   - Multiply resolution until time/consistency constraints\n\n   Helper Functions:\n   - spectral_smoothing(h, alpha): Apply low-pass filter in frequency domain\n   - population_diversity(pop): Compute diversity metric based on spectral distances\n   - projection_to_feasible(h): Enforce 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and \u2211h_i=n/2\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Default Settings:\n   - Initial population size: 10\n   - GP kernel: Mat\u00e9rn nu=5/2\n   - Acquisition EI threshold: 0.001\n   - Smoothing alpha: 0.2\n   - Temperature schedule: T = 10 * (0.8)^(generation)\n\n   Adaptive Strategies:\n   - Low-dimensional restarts: Switch to 32-point grid if plateau detected\n   - Modified initialization: Change spectral forcing pattern if diversity drops\n   - Progressive smoothing: Decrease alpha by 0.1 every 20 evaluations\n   - Dynamic population growth: Increase population size by 2x when improvement stalled\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   Time Estimation:\n   - Baseline eval: O(n^2) correlation + O(n log n) FFT operations\n   - n=64: ~0.005s op/eval\n   - n=128: ~0.040s op/eval\n   - n=256: ~0.320s op/eval\n   - n=512: ~2.56s op/eval\n\n   Budget Allocation:\n   - Phase 1: 10% budget (initialization)\n   - Phase 2: 40-60% budget (BO)\n   - Phase 3: 5-10% budget (local refinement)\n   - Phase 4: 20-30% budget (resolution change)\n\n   Deadline Handling:\n   - Monitor remaining time after each evaluation\n   - Shrink GP covariance observations as fallback\n   - Maintain minimal population of 5 candidates in crisis mode\n   - Fallback: Continue with best candidate found\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Post-Processing Steps:\n   1. Clip and project final candidate\n   2. Validate constraints with tolerance:\n        abs(sum(h) - n/2) < 1e-6\n        0 <= min(h) and max(h) <= 1+1e-6\n   3. Re-evaluate C5 with original function\n   4. Maintain history of best candidates\n\n   Return Format:\n   - h_values: 1D array of floats, length n\n   - c5_bound: computed objective value\n   - n_points: length of h_values\n\n   Validation Assertions:\n   ```python\n   assert n_points >= 32 and n_points <= 1024\n   assert all(0 <= x <= 1 for x in h_values)\n   assert abs(sum(h_values) - n_points/2) < 1e-5\n   assert c5_bound == compute_c5(h_values, 2.0/n_points)\n   ```",
